.TH EUCLID-CLI-EES-ACK-EVENTS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ees-ack-events \- acknowledge claimed events, deleting them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ees ack-events
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-event\-ids | \-i
.I LIST
.SH DESCRIPTION
Deletes events that were claimed by
.BR euclid-cli-ees-receive-events (1),
which is what "processed" means here: until an event is acknowledged it
is only leased, and it comes back for another claimer when that lease
runs out.
.PP
Acknowledge after the work is done, not when the event arrives. That
ordering is the whole point of the lease: a consumer that dies between
receiving an event and finishing with it leaves the event to be
redelivered, whereas one that acknowledged first would have thrown it
away.
.PP
An event that is no longer there is not an error. A redelivery
acknowledged twice, and one whose seven-day retention ran out, both end
up here and mean the same thing to the caller \- so the answer reports
how many were actually deleted
.RI ( acknowledged )
and how many are still held for this subscriber
.RI ( waiting ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Subscriber name the events were claimed under. Required.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-event\-ids " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated event IDs, taken from the
.I eventId
of a
.B receive-events
answer. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The call succeeded, including when none of the IDs matched anything.
Counts are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, an empty
.BR \-\-event\-ids ,
no session, or the server rejected the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees ack-events --name invoice-import \e
    --event-ids b1b24dcc-3c78-441e-ab8a-8054a1ceda12
.fi
.RE
.PP
Receiving and acknowledging in one step, for a consumer that only
reads:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees receive-events --name audit-tail --acknowledge
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-receive-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-list-subscriptions (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
